@meta
id: 30-16
title: Scenario — the gateway receipt
chapter: 30
type: scenario
skills: ctags, diagnostics, rename-symbol, cfdo
par: 295
minutes: 18
vscode: Problems panel, F12, F2, and a careful look at the diff
vimrc: set errorformat=%-G\ %.%#,%f(%l\\,%c):\ error\ %m hidden
open: src/domain/order/order.resolver.ts

@brief
A ticket, three jobs, no language server.

    ORD-733  The payments gateway now returns a receipt reference with
             every refund and finance need it stored. The type-check is
             also red, and while you are in there, PaymentService.refund
             is misnamed: it does not send money, it asks the gateway to
             issue a refund.

  1. **The red build.** `tsc.log` is provided, two errors. Fix both.

  2. **The receipt.** The gateway response type is `RefundResponse`. You
     do not know which file it is in; the provided `tags` file does. Add

         receiptId: string;

     to its `data` object, under `reference`. Then have
     `PaymentService.refund` return `response.data.receiptId` rather
     than the whole `data` object.

  3. **The rename.** `PaymentService.refund` becomes `issueRefund`,
     definition and callers. The endpoint `'/refunds'` and the routing
     key `'payment.refund'` in the producer are wire contracts and do
     not change.

Do the rename last. It is the only job that makes the tags file stale,
and jobs one and two both want to use it.

@keys
| `:cfile file` `:cn` | the errors, one after another |
| `:tag name` | to a definition anywhere in the tree |
| `Ctrl-T` | back where you came from |
| `:vimgrep /\<name\>/ src/**/*.ts` | every whole-word hit |
| `:cfdo %s/old/new/g \| update` | rename across the files that matched |
| `%s/pat/new/ge` | the `e` flag: no error in files with no match |

@hints
Take the errors first: `:cfile tsc.log` lands on the first, `:cn` on
the second. Neither needs more than a few keystrokes.
--
`:tag RefundResponse` goes to the type. `o` under the `reference` line
supplies the indentation for you, and `Ctrl-T` brings you back to the
service to change the `return`.
--
For the rename, `\<refund\>` never matches `refunds`, so the endpoint
looks after itself. The routing key does not: `'payment.refund'` has a
word boundary in exactly the wrong place.
--
Rename everything, then put the routing key back with a second pass —
the same two-step as the rename lesson. Finish with `:wqa`, because the
type file was edited by hand and is not in any list.

@solution
    :cfile tsc.log<Enter>  ciw id <Esc>  :cn<Enter>  f' x f' x
    :tag RefundResponse<Enter>  /reference<Enter>  o receiptId: string; <Esc>
    Ctrl-T  /response\.data<Enter>  f; i .receiptId <Esc>
    :vimgrep /\<refund\>/ src/**/*.ts<Enter>
    :cfdo %s/\<refund\>/issueRefund/g | update<Enter>
    :cfdo %s/'payment\.issueRefund'/'payment.refund'/ge | update<Enter>
    :wqa<Enter>

Around 250 keystrokes, and not one of them is a file you had to go and
find by hand.

The order matters more than the keys. Tags first, rename last: a rename
moves every definition it touches, and until `ctags -R src` runs again
the index points at lines that have changed underneath it. Vim degrades
politely — the tag address is a search pattern, so a definition that has
only moved is still found, and one that has been renamed is not found at
all. That failure is loud, which is the best kind.

The three tools each answered a different question, and it is worth
naming them:

  · the quickfix list answered "what is broken" — and it came from a
    file, so it works the same from a saved log, a CI artefact, `:make`
    or a server plugin
  · the tags file answered "where does this live" — from an index a
    shell command wrote in a second
  · `:vimgrep` and `:cfdo` answered "everywhere this name appears" —
    and needed you to know the difference between a name and a string,
    which is the one thing they cannot work out for themselves

That is go-to-definition, the problems panel and rename symbol, on a
machine with no plugins, no server and no network. Set up a language
server in the projects where it earns its keep. Keep these, because
they are what is there when it does not.

